# Previewing an edit
@scenario tutorial_lesson_27
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/27_editor_workflows.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt target]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Preview lets you inspect an individual script edit. A full run also checks the commands that execute before that point.
# region target
[show ginger beside_code embarrassed]
ginger: {b}Preview target.{/b} Check the expression shown on this line.
# endregion
ginger: In the show command just above this line, change embarrassed to annoyed. Place the cursor on the dialogue and compare the result in Puppeteer.
ginger: Now run the lesson from the beginning. Check that the sprite starts with the earlier pose and changes on the expected line.
ginger: Cursor preview is useful for a precise edit. A run from the beginning supplies all the instructions preceding that edit. Write down which setup each test actually exercises.
ginger: For this pose test, use the dialogue line as a checkpoint. Record the current speaker, expression and position so a change in one is easy to identify.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Compare two preview entry points -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: What should you compare when checking an edit?
> The cursor beat in the editor and the runtime scene state. -> correct
> Only the file modification time. -> retry
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: The modification time confirms that the file was saved. It doesn't identify which line or visual state was displayed.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: Compare the same script line and the state that led to it. Preview checks the frame; a full run checks the transition into it.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_27]
[flag tutorial_done_27]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_6]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/27_editor_workflows.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt target]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Change embarrassed to annoyed in the command above Preview target. Start the preview before the command, advance to the line, and note the expression.
ginger: Repeat from the topic menu and stop at the same text. Matching text identifies the checkpoint. Compare the pose as a separate observation.
ginger: If the previews disagree, move the starting point backwards one setup command at a time. The first position that restores the expected result tells you which prerequisite to inspect.
ginger: After a successful cursor test, make one full run through the preceding choice or setup and out through the following choice. This also checks how the edited line behaves in context.
@label practice_question
ginger: The edited pose appears when starting before show but not when starting after it. What does that tell you?
> That checkpoint depends on the show command. -> practice_result
> The speaker text needs rewriting. -> practice_hint
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: The words at the checkpoint were the same in both runs. The difference is whether the pose assignment executed.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: Starting before the command exercises the assignment. Record that prerequisite so the preview route and full run can be compared fairly.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
